WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:09.025
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:14.706
Unit six: Neuroeconomics. Today you will weigh choices using favoring X over Y.

4
00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:17.646
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.357
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:23.566
Neuroeconomics.

7
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.686
Neuroeconomics means the study of how the brain makes economic decisions.

8
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:32.479
Neuroeconomics uses brain scans to study spending.

9
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:35.910
Your turn. Say: Neuroeconomics.

10
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.058
Neuroeconomics.

11
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:42.120
Reward system.

12
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:47.380
Reward system means brain circuits that produce pleasure and motivate behaviour.

13
00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:50.854
The reward system responds strongly to novelty.

14
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.079
Your turn. Say: Reward system.

15
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:58.132
Reward system.

16
00:00:58.883 --> 00:00:59.861
Dopamine.

17
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.707
Dopamine means a brain chemical linked to motivation and anticipation.

18
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:07.792
Dopamine rises when we expect a reward.

19
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:10.931
Your turn. Say: Dopamine.

20
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:14.814
Dopamine.

21
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.911
Risk perception.

22
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:21.484
Risk perception means how dangerous a choice feels to a person.

23
00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:25.186
Risk perception is shaped by emotion as well as numbers.

24
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:28.552
Your turn. Say: Risk perception.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.796
Risk perception.

26
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.271
Decision-making circuit.

27
00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:41.371
Decision-making circuit means linked brain regions that evaluate and choose among options.

28
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:45.225
A decision-making circuit weighs value against cost.

29
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:49.572
Your turn. Say: Decision-making circuit.

30
00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:54.003
Decision-making circuit.

31
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.134
Value computation.

32
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.390
Value computation means the brain's calculation of how much an option is worth.

33
00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:05.005
Value computation integrates price, effort and delay.

34
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:08.803
Your turn. Say: Value computation.

35
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.283
Value computation.

36
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:15.331
Impulsivity.

37
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:20.336
Impulsivity means acting quickly without considering consequences.

38
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.263
Impulsivity makes saving difficult.

39
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.897
Your turn. Say: Impulsivity.

40
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:30.943
Impulsivity.

41
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:33.346
Delayed gratification.

42
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:39.612
Delayed gratification means waiting for a larger reward instead of taking a smaller one now.

43
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:43.760
Delayed gratification predicts success in many studies.

44
00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.258
Your turn. Say: Delayed gratification.

45
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.925
Delayed gratification.

46
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:56.477
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.317 --> 00:03:03.173
Visal, I know I should save my salary, yet I keep spending it on small treats. Why?

48
00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:14.120
The brain sometimes favors immediate reward over delayed gratification, because the reward system responds strongly to what is near.

49
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:17.135
So it's not a lack of willpower?

50
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:29.470
Partly biology. Value computation discounts future benefits, so people often favor a small prize today over a larger one next year.

51
00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:31.944
Can we resist it?

52
00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:39.069
Yes. Automatic saving favors the long term by removing the daily decision.

53
00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:41.676
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:45.550
Grammar focus: Favoring X over Y.

55
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.013
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.717 --> 00:03:51.551
The brain sometimes favors immediate reward over delayed gratification.

57
00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:56.690
Many people favor a small prize today over a larger one next year.

58
00:03:57.583 --> 00:04:01.789
Automatic saving favors the long term over momentary impulse.

59
00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:12.703
Use favor / prefer / prioritise X over Y. 'Over' shows the rejected option. Do not use 'than' after favor: favors X over Y (not 'favors X than Y').

60
00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:17.658
Be careful! Don't say: The brain favors reward than delay.

61
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.679
Say: The brain favors reward over delay.

62
00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:24.538
favor X over Y.

63
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:27.974
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:29.884
The Pull of Now.

65
00:04:30.184 --> 00:04:35.051
Neuroeconomics combines neuroscience with economics to explain how decisions are made.

66
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:43.186
Brain scans show that the reward system, driven by dopamine, responds more strongly to immediate rewards than to future ones.

67
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:51.215
As a result, the brain sometimes favors immediate reward over delayed gratification, even when waiting would pay more.

68
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:58.008
Impulsivity varies between individuals and is influenced by stress, sleep and environment.

69
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.144
Policy design can help by making the beneficial option automatic, so that the long term is favored without effort.

70
00:05:06.233 --> 00:05:10.316
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:14.612
The brain favors immediate reward blank delayed gratification.

72
00:05:14.912 --> 00:05:17.846
A, over. B, than. C, to.

73
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.156
The answer is A: over.

74
00:05:24.317 --> 00:05:26.990
A brain chemical linked to motivation is blank.

75
00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:30.769
A, dopamine. B, impulsivity. C, circuit.

76
00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.194
The answer is A: dopamine.

77
00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.733
Waiting for a larger reward is blank.

78
00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:45.857
A, impulsivity. B, delayed gratification. C, value computation.

79
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.294
The answer is B: delayed gratification.

80
00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:55.183
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:55.483 --> 00:06:02.781
A, She favors safety than speed.. B, She favors safety over speed.. C, She favors safety to over speed..

82
00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:09.819
The answer is B: She favors safety over speed..

83
00:06:10.983 --> 00:06:15.026
According to the reading, policy can help by making the beneficial option…

84
00:06:15.326 --> 00:06:19.398
A, optional. B, automatic. C, expensive.

85
00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:24.968
The answer is B: automatic.

86
00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:29.005
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:34.694
Why do you think people sometimes choose immediate rewards over long-term benefits?

88
00:06:51.483 --> 00:06:52.960
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:53.260 --> 00:07:18.267
People often choose immediate rewards because the brain weights the present more heavily than the future. A small pleasure today feels certain, whereas a larger benefit next year feels distant and uncertain. Stress and tiredness make this worse. Setting up automatic habits, such as saving on payday, helps the long term win over the moment.

90
00:07:18.567 --> 00:07:20.184
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:21.083 --> 00:07:25.326
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and favoring x over y.

92
00:07:25.626 --> 00:07:30.577
Today's value is self-discipline. Control your habits, so your habits do not control you.

93
00:07:30.877 --> 00:07:33.379
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:33.679 --> 00:07:36.795
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

